<interrogationes unitas="imago_png">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I save an Imago to a PNG file?
    <responsum symbolum="imago_png_scribere PngFructus">Call
      imago_png_scribere(imago, via, piscina) with a NUL-terminated
      path and check successus on the PngFructus it returns. On
      failure error usually names the cause (for example Imago abest,
      Imago vacua, Via abest, Plagula aperiri non potest, Scriptio
      incompleta); it is a chorda, so print it with %.*s, not %s. On
      success datum and mensura are the bytes that were written, held
      in the piscina. Only the fwrite count is checked: the result of
      fclose is ignored, so an error that appears only when the file
      is flushed at close still reports success.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I get the PNG bytes in memory without touching the filesystem?
    <responsum symbolum="imago_png_codificare piscina_reficere">imago_png_codificare(imago,
      piscina) is pure: no file is read or written. The bytes
      (datum, mensura) are allocated in the piscina and need no
      freeing; they live until the piscina is destroyed, emptied, or
      rolled back past them with piscina_reficere. imago_png_scribere is only this call followed by
      lectiones_fopen, one fwrite and fclose.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I read or decode a PNG with imago_png?
    <responsum symbolum="imago_caricare_ex_file imago_caricare_ex_memoria">No,
      it only encodes. Decoding goes through imago_caricare_ex_file
      or imago_caricare_ex_memoria (lib/imago.c, vendored stb_image).
      The asymmetry is deliberate: the encoder writes one fixed
      layout, while a decoder must accept every bit depth, palette,
      interlace mode and filter type.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what pixel layout does imago_png_codificare expect?
    <responsum symbolum="imago_png_codificare Imago" via="include/imago_typus.h">An
      Imago: RGBA, IV bytes per pixel, NOT premultiplied, rows packed
      with no stride or padding (row y starts at pixela + y *
      latitudo * IV). There is no RGB or grayscale input; convert to
      RGBA first. imago_typus.h holds the type alone, so including
      imago_png.h does not pull in the decoder.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why are the PNG files larger than other encoders produce?
    <responsum symbolum="imago_png_codificare" via="lib/imago_png.worklog.md">Every
      row uses filter None, there is one IDAT and no interlacing. The
      filters PNG offers improve compression, mostly on photographic
      content; screenshots (flat regions) barely suffer. In exchange
      the code is small and the output is deterministic: the same
      image always gives the same bytes, which tests depend on.
      Adding filters later changes only the implementation, not the
      interface.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I pass a NIHIL piscina or an empty image to the encoder?
    <responsum symbolum="imago_png_codificare piscina_allocare">You get successus FALSUM
      either way. A NIHIL image or NIHIL pixela gives Imago abest, a
      zero latitudo or altitudo gives Imago vacua. A NIHIL piscina also gives
      successus FALSUM, but error is EMPTY, because there is nowhere
      to allocate the message, despite the header's promise that error
      is always named. The encoder's own buffers come from
      piscina_allocare, which is fatal on failure, so running out of
      memory exits the process and the Allocatio fracta messages in
      the code never appear.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I encode a very large image (tens of thousands of pixels on a side)?
    <responsum symbolum="imago_png_codificare">Only while the raw
      scanline buffer, (1 + latitudo * IV) * altitudo bytes, stays
      well below 4 GiB (the compressed stream and the file size are
      i32 as well). Every size and offset is computed in i32 (unsigned
      32 bit) with no overflow check, so a larger image wraps: for
      32768 x 32769 the buffer comes out about 160 KiB and the row
      copy writes far past it. There is no refusal for oversized
      input; check the dimensions yourself before calling.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
